---
/**
 * XGlyph — the X mark, drawn by hand on the icon system's 16-unit grid.
 *
 * It replaces the literal letter "X" that used to sit in the footer's social
 * row, which is the one label on this site that could not do its job: a lone
 * "X" in 11px mono reads as a close button, a multiplication sign or a
 * placeholder before it reads as the name of a company. "GitHub" beside it has
 * no such problem, which is why that one is still a word — a logo earns its
 * place when the word cannot be read, not because its neighbour has one.
 *
 * Nothing here is fetched. The site loads no third-party asset (`/privacy` is
 * built around that claim), so the mark is drawn in `currentColor` and inherits
 * the row's own ink, hover and theme.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The construction, which is not the X you would guess at.
 *
 * The mark is a **double-struck X**, the descendant of its Unicode namesake
 * (MATHEMATICAL DOUBLE-STRUCK CAPITAL X). It is three strokes, not two:
 *
 * 1. the "\" is a *ribbon* — two parallel walls closed by a flat cap at each
 *    end, with a counter running between them;
 * 2. the "/" is a single stroke of the same weight crossing both walls;
 * 3. the counter punches through the "/" as well, so the "/" breaks where it
 *    passes the ribbon and reads as running behind it.
 *
 * **Every end is cut horizontally**, parallel to the baseline rather than
 * square to its own stroke. That is the detail that makes it this mark instead
 * of a dismiss icon, and it is also why this cannot be a stroked path like the
 * rest of the icon set: `stroke-linecap` can give a butt cut or a round one,
 * never a horizontal one on a diagonal. So it is drawn as ink — one filled
 * path, the outline and its counter — and the weight is what carries it back
 * into the set.
 *
 * **Weight: 1.25 units, perpendicular, on all three strokes.** That is the
 * number `ShotActions.astro` and `ThemeToggle.astro` set as `stroke-width`, now
 * spelled as ink instead of as a stroke, so the glyph sits at the weight of the
 * moon two items along instead of shouting over it. The real mark is lighter
 * than this against its own box (about 0.87 units at this scale) and looked
 * spindly next to a 1.25 stroke; 1.35 and up closed the counter at 14px. Both
 * were rasterised at 1x before this one was picked.
 *
 * **The angles fall out of the geometry rather than being chosen.** Both
 * strokes run corner to corner of a 13-unit ink box, so the ribbon — which is
 * two walls plus a counter wide — has to lie shallower than the single "/" to
 * fit: 32.1 degrees off vertical against 41.1. The real mark is 34.5 against
 * 40.7 for exactly the same reason, and the small difference is our heavier
 * stroke widening the ribbon. An X drawn symmetrically here would be a
 * different letter.
 *
 * Ink box 1.5 to 14.5 on both axes, centred in the 16-unit box, which is the
 * density `ThemeToggle.astro`'s sun already sits at.
 *
 * The counter's flat caps are inset from the ends by one stroke weight, 1.25,
 * measured vertically — the proportion the real mark uses, where the inset and
 * the stroke are the same number to three significant figures.
 */
---

{/*
  Hidden from the accessibility tree on purpose: the anchor around it carries
  `aria-label="X"`, and a name here would announce the destination twice.
*/}
<svg class="xglyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  {/*
    One path, two subpaths: the outline, then the counter. `evenodd` rather
    than the default, so the counter stays a hole whichever way its points end
    up ordered — the alternative is an invariant about winding direction that
    nothing in the file would catch.
  */}
  <path
    d="M9.755 6.939 L14.5 1.5 L12.841 1.5 L9.061 5.834 L6.341 1.5 L1.5 1.5 L6.245 9.061 L1.5 14.5 L3.159 14.5 L6.939 10.166 L9.659 14.5 L14.5 14.5 Z M3.76 2.75 L5.649 2.75 L12.24 13.25 L10.351 13.25 Z"
    fill="currentColor"
    fill-rule="evenodd"></path>
</svg>

<style>
  /* A 16-unit icon set at 14px sits at the weight of the 11px mono beside it
     instead of shouting over it. Same call as `ShotActions.astro` and
     `ThemeToggle.astro`, and the size the weight above was tuned against. */
  .xglyph {
    display: block;
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
  }
</style>
